 First, we compute the solutions of $x^2+y^2=50.$ List positive factorizations of 50, and see if switching the order gives us a solution. \[50=1\cdot 50=5\cdot 10 \Rightarrow (x,y)=\pm (1,\pm 7),\ \pm (5,\pm 3). \]Now, for each solution, use the corresponding $x$ and $y$ to determine the line that contains the corresponding ordered pair, and do the opposite for the other equation. This gives \[a\pm 7b=\pm a\pm 3b=1.\] We have the following 8 systems of equations to solve: \[\begin{aligned} a+7b&=1 \\ a+3b&=1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a+7b&=1 \\ a+3b&=-1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a+7b&=-1 \\ a+3b&=1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a+7b&=-1 \\ a+3b&=-1.  \end{aligned}\;\\[5mm]
\begin{aligned} a-7b&=1 \\ a-3b&=1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a-7b&=1 \\ a-3b&=-1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a-7b&=-1 \\ a-3b&=1.  \end{aligned}\;\;\;
\begin{aligned} a-7b&=-1 \\ a-3b&=-1.  \end{aligned}\] Adding the equations in a system gives $4b=0\,$ or $4a=2\,$ in each case, so $b=0\,$ or $a=\frac 12.\,$ Then $a=1,\,$ $b=0,\,$ $a=-1,\,$ and $b=0$ are solutions. If $b=0,\,$ then either $x=\pm 1\,$ and $y=\pm 7,\,$ or $x=\pm 7\,$ and $y=\pm 1,\,$ so there are 8 corresponding equations of the form $ax=\pm 7$ and $ax=\pm 1,\,$ which give us 16 more solutions. We find one more solution, $\left(\frac 12,\, \frac 1{14}\right).$ Therefore, there are $\boxed{17}$ corresponding ordered pairs $(a,b).\,$ </think> <answer>$\textbf{(A) }17$</answer>

